      "problem": "Factor $486-6{x}^{4}$ completely.",
      "worked_solution": "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $6.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $6,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n486-6{x}^{4}=6\\cdot 81-6\\cdot {x}^{4}=6(81-{x}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n6(81-{x}^{4})=6({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n6({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})=6(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2}) & =6(9+{x}^{2})({3}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =6(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
